The lsp knows of the existence of my `enum struct FOOBAR` and can find most references across files. 
```cpp
enum struct FOOBAR {
    foo,
    bar,
};
template<FOOBAR one, FOOBAR two>
constexpr void func() {
    if (one == FOOBAR::foo) return;
}
```
Using neovim I can get a list of references with:
[`:lua vim.lsp.buf.references()`](https://neovim.io/doc/user/lsp.html#vim.lsp.buf.references())

Calling this on any occurrence of `FOOBAR` returns a list with the definition and the if statement, but not either of the template args.
I can even call it on the template arg and it will show me the other references but not the template args.

The issue is that when I use neovims [rename()](https://neovim.io/doc/user/lsp.html#vim.lsp.buf.rename()) feature, I can rename all occurrences of `FOOBAR` except those in template parameters in *other* files (occurrences in the same file seem to be caught).

