I think this issue will be a bit hard to explain, but let's give it a try. It took me a while to understand why clangd was crashing when a template argument had to be a template template. I guess a code snippet will explain this better.

```cpp
// Let's suppose that, for a very good reason, we need a base class that requires
// a template template.
template <template <typename> typename T>
struct Base
{
  // Now we can create a generic foo method dependent on T and K.
  template <typename K>
  T<K> foo(const K&);
};

// Now let's suppose that we have a data structure that defines a nested
// template structure.
struct MyStruct
{
  template <typename T>
  struct MyInnerStruct
  {
   ...
  };
};

// We can now define a derived class that passes the inner struct
// template to the base class.
// We'll need to use the template keyword to correctly expose the template.
struct Derived : Base<MyStruct::template MyInnerStruct>
{
};

// Up to this point everything is fine. However, when we try to define a
/// template of a derived class, clangd will crash as soon as 
// `T::template MyInnerStruct` is written.
// template <typename T>
